
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:credential@

%9Parameters:%9

    PASSWD:     Sets the master password for this session and unlocks
                credentials that were encrypted by an earlier version.
    LIST:       Lists the stored credentials. SASL user names are shown;
                passwords and autosendcmd values are shown as '***'.
    STATUS:     Displays the storage mode, the external file name, whether
                encryption is enabled and whether a master password is set.
    MIGRATE:    CONFIG moves credentials from the external file back into
                the main configuration file. EXTERNAL is refused (see below).
    ENCRYPT:    Refused (see below).
    DECRYPT:    Stores credentials that are encrypted in plain text again.
    RELOAD:     Reloads the credentials from the external file.

    Without any parameter, a short summary of the commands and settings is
    displayed.

%9Description:%9

    Shows and manages the credentials of your networks and servers: server
    passwords, the SASL user name and password of a network, and
    autosendcmd values that contain a login. They are added and removed
    with the normal commands, NETWORK ADD -sasl_username and
    -sasl_password, or SERVER ADD -password.

    Credentials are kept in ~/.erssi/config, which erssi writes readable
    only by you (mode 0600).

    Encryption and the external credentials file are disabled in this
    version: encryption never reached the configuration file (passwords
    stayed in plain text in it), the external mode put them back into it,
    and a wrong master password could delete stored credentials. Turning
    them on - with ENCRYPT, MIGRATE EXTERNAL or /SET - is refused with a
    message. A configuration that already uses them keeps working, so
    nothing stored before is lost; use DECRYPT and MIGRATE CONFIG to go
    back to plain credentials in the configuration file.

    When erssi starts with a configuration that still has
    credential_config_encrypt ON or credential_storage_mode external, or
    that holds encrypted values, it says so once in the status window:

      - What is protected: ~/.erssi/config is a plain-text file, readable
        only by you once erssi has written it (mode 0600; the warning says
        when it still has another mode). Only the values that the old encryption
        encrypted are encrypted in it; everything else is plain text.
        Credentials in the external file are plain text there unless the
        old encryption was on.
      - To go back to plain storage: /CREDENTIAL PASSWD <master password>
        and /CREDENTIAL DECRYPT (while credential_config_encrypt is ON),
        /CREDENTIAL MIGRATE CONFIG (while credential_storage_mode is
        external), then /SAVE. A value left encrypted while the encryption
        is OFF is no longer decrypted: enter that password again (NETWORK
        ADD -sasl_password, SERVER ADD -password), then /SAVE.
      - To keep passwords out of the file: log in with a client
        certificate (NETWORK ADD -sasl_mechanism EXTERNAL, SERVER ADD
        -tls_cert), or keep your home directory on an encrypted disk.

    The external credentials file may be a symbolic link (kept with your
    dotfiles or on an encrypted volume): it is read and written where the
    link points, and that must be a regular file of your own - a FIFO, a
    directory or another user's file is neither read nor written. When
    the file cannot be read or written, erssi says so with the reason
    (at the start, with the warning above); a link that leads to no file
    (a volume not mounted) is such a file, not a missing one. Nothing is
    lost then: after a failed read the file is not written until RELOAD
    reads it, a password you enter stays in ~/.erssi/config until the
    external file holds it, and MIGRATE CONFIG that fails leaves
    credential_storage_mode external. With no external file at all,
    MIGRATE CONFIG has nothing to move and just switches to the
    configuration file. After a migration the external file is removed;
    when it is a link, the file it points to is emptied and the link
    removed. /SAVE does not write the credentials loaded from the
    external file into ~/.erssi/config - except while that file cannot
    be read or written: then a network or server you change keeps its
    passwords in ~/.erssi/config until RELOAD succeeds, so nothing is
    lost. RELOAD gives the reloaded credentials to the networks.

    A value encrypted by this version starts with erssi-enc1: and is
    AES-256-GCM with a key made from the master password, so a value that
    was changed, or a wrong master password, fails to decrypt (with a
    warning) instead of giving a wrong password. Values encrypted by
    versions before 2026.10.3 have no such prefix and are still read, but
    that older form cannot show that it was changed: erssi warns once when
    it reads one. What becomes of such a value:

      - With credential_config_encrypt ON and the credentials in the
        configuration file, /CREDENTIAL PASSWD decrypts the encrypted
        values in the configuration in memory, old and current form
        alike, and /SAVE then writes them in plain text (see above).
      - A value that is still in the old form when you /SAVE, with the
        credentials in the configuration file and the master password
        set, is written again in the current form, but only when a value
        already in the current form decrypts with the same master
        password: a wrong master password can decrypt an old value to
        random text, which must not replace it. Otherwise it stays as it
        is.
      - Values in the external credentials file are never written again
        in the current form.

    Entering the password again (NETWORK ADD -sasl_password, SERVER ADD
    -password) replaces an old value with the plain password.

    Settings:

        credential_storage_mode (default: config)
        credential_external_file (default: .credentials)
        credential_config_encrypt (default: OFF)

%9Examples:%9

    /CREDENTIAL STATUS
    /CREDENTIAL LIST
    /CREDENTIAL DECRYPT
    /CREDENTIAL MIGRATE CONFIG

%9See also:%9 NETWORK, SAVE, SERVER, SET
